## Example

Let $B=0$ on a finite-dimensional $k$-vector space $V$ and hence $Q=0$. The two quotient presentations identify $\mathrm{Cl}(V,0)$ canonically with $\Lambda V$. If $e_1,\ldots,e_n$ is an ordered basis, their common basis is $1$ and the increasing square-free monomials. The form is degenerate whenever $V\ne0$.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** A field $k$ of characteristic not $2$, a finite-dimensional vector space $V$ with ordered basis $e_1,\ldots,e_n$, and $B=Q=0$.

[F1] The Clifford algebra is the tensor-algebra quotient by the ideal generated by $v^2-Q(v)1$ ([[def-clifford-algebra-of-a-quadratic-space]]).

[F2] The exterior algebra is the tensor-algebra quotient by the ideal generated by $v^2$ ([[def-exterior-algebra-of-a-finite-free-module]]).

[F3] The ordered square-free Clifford monomials form a basis ([[thm-clifford-pbw-basis-and-associated-graded]]).

[F4] The increasing exterior monomials form a basis ([[lem-exterior-algebra-basis-monomials]]).

## Verification

**Proof technique:** direct.

1.1 Since $Q(v)=0$ for every $v$, [F1] and [F2] quotient $T_k(V)$ by the same two-sided ideal. The map $[t]_{\mathrm{Cl}}\mapsto[t]_{\Lambda}$ is therefore well defined, preserves the induced algebra operations, and has the inverse $[t]_{\Lambda}\mapsto[t]_{\mathrm{Cl}}$. [given, F1, F2, construct]

2.1 By [F3] and [F4], the same ordered square-free monomials are bases on the two sides; for $n=0$ both lists consist only of $1$. Thus the canonical presentation isomorphism has the asserted basis description, including the degenerate case. [given, F3, F4, step 1.1] ∎
